Protocol Reference: Redis (Remote Dictionary Server)
Overview
Redis is an open-source, in-memory key-value data store used primarily as a database, cache, and message broker.

Default Port: 6379 (TCP).

Key Feature: Because data resides in RAM, read/write operations are extremely fast compared to disk-based databases like MySQL or PostgreSQL.

Security Implications
Unauthenticated Access: By default, older versions or misconfigured instances of Redis do not enforce password authentication when bound to external interfaces.

Potential Risks: Unauthenticated users can read sensitive stored data, write files to disk (e.g., SSH authorized keys or web shells), or cause Denial of Service (DoS) using commands like FLUSHALL.

Command Cheat Sheet
Network Reconnaissance
Bash
# Scan for Redis port explicitly and retrieve service information
nmap -p 6379 -sV <TARGET_IP>
Redis Enumeration (redis-cli)
Bash
# Connect to a remote Redis instance
redis-cli -h <TARGET_IP> -p 6379

# Connect without specifying port (defaults to 6379)
redis-cli -h <TARGET_IP>
Essential Redis Commands
Bash
127.0.0.1:6379> INFO             # Display server info, version, and DB statistics
127.0.0.1:6379> SELECT <index>   # Switch to a specific database (e.g., SELECT 0)
127.0.0.1:6379> KEYS *           # List all keys in the selected database
127.0.0.1:6379> GET <key_name>   # Fetch the value stored inside a specific key
127.0.0.1:6379> DBSIZE           # Get total number of keys in current database
127.0.0.1:6379> EXIT             # Quit the redis-cli session
